---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { CONTACT_EMAIL } from '../site/settings.js';
---

<Base title="About" description="What the Bradbury Challenge is, where the readings come from and how they are chosen.">
  <article class="prose">
    <h1 class="page-title">About</h1>

    <p>
      Ray Bradbury liked to give young writers a simple piece of advice: for a thousand nights, before going to sleep,
      read one short story, one poem and one essay. Do that, he said, and you will have filled your head with other
      people's ideas, images and ways of putting a sentence together, which is where your own will come from. He gave
      the advice in talks to students for years, most famously at a writers' symposium in 2001, and it has been
      passed around ever since by readers who simply wanted a good reading habit.
    </p>
    <p>
      Bradbury Challenge makes that habit easy to keep. Every day at midnight, Lisbon time, there is a new poem, a new
      essay and a new short story. You do not need an account. You read the
      texts where their publishers put them, and you can tick each one off as you go; your progress stays in your own
      browser.
    </p>

    <h2>Where the texts come from</h2>
    <p>
      Every reading is free to read in full, with no paywall and no subscription. We do not copy texts onto this site:
      each reading is a link to the page where it was published, so the writers and editors who made it get the
      reader. The sources are:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Poems:</strong> Representative Poetry Online, the anthology edited at the University of Toronto; the
        Poetry Archive, which records poets reading their own work; Poetry International in Rotterdam; the poems
        published in The New York Review of Books; and Literary Hub.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Essays:</strong> the literary essays of the Los Angeles Review of Books; classic essays in the public
        domain on Wikisource, from Montaigne and Bacon to Hazlitt, Emerson, Woolf and Chesterton; and the essays of The
        New York Review of Books that are free to read.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Short stories:</strong> the Library of Short Stories, a collection of classic stories in the public
        domain, and the stories published by Literary Hub.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Some of the classic texts were written in other languages; they appear here in English translations, with the
      translator named.
    </p>

    <h2>How the readings are chosen</h2>
    <p>
      The catalogue holds thousands of texts, and a new day's selection is drawn from it automatically. The order is
      pseudo-random but fixed: once a day has its three readings, they never change, so the page for any past day
      always shows what was read that day. No text comes back while there are texts that have not yet appeared, and
      when a category has been read through, nothing returns that appeared in the previous twelve months. The draw also
      takes turns between sources, so that a single magazine does not fill a week.
    </p>
    <p>
      Days are not revealed in advance. If today's readings are not up yet, which can happen for a few minutes after
      midnight, the home page shows yesterday's and says so.
    </p>

    <h2>Rights holders</h2>
    <p>
      If you publish one of the texts we link to and would rather we did not, write to{' '}
      <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a> and we will remove the link.
    </p>

    <h2>Not affiliated</h2>
    <p>
      Bradbury Challenge is an independent project. It is named after Ray Bradbury's advice, but it is not connected to
      Ray Bradbury's estate or to any of the publications it links to.
    </p>
  </article>
</Base>
